\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Gaussian elimination at every prime}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{Which parts of Gaussian elimination depend on continuous quantities? The
[arithmetic families](fdip-001D) place the Clifford data over every ring in
which #{2} is a unit, and the [bad fibers](fdip-000W) separate the domains
of the form, the elimination, and the trace ratio. The Gaussian law asks a
further question. A moment can lie in #{\mathbb Q} while the constant that
normalizes the real density does not.}

\p{Take the unit-weight four-cycle with mass squared equal to #{1}. In vertex
order #{(0,1,2,3)} its precision is
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1&0&-1\\-1&3&-1&0\\0&-1&3&-1\\-1&0&-1&3\end{pmatrix},}
so #{\det Q=45}. For these 4 coordinates the real normalizer is
##{(2\pi)^{2}(\det Q)^{-1/2},}
as in the [finite positive Gaussian normalization](fdip-002T). That
quantity involves both #{\pi} and #{\sqrt{45}}. Retain the adjacent
vertices #{R=\{0,1\}} and eliminate #{U=\{2,3\}}. This split is not the
opposite-vertex split of the [four-cycle elimination](fdip-000F). The
\vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D} and one moment computed in the next
section are
##{S=\begin{pmatrix}21/8&-9/8\\-9/8&21/8\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\omega_Q(x_2^2x_3x_0+x_2^3x_1)=32/75.}
Both lie in #{\mathbb Q}. In this example the continuous quantity is the
real normalizer.}

\p{The next section defines the moment rule over a ring and proves that
elimination of those moments is the Schur complement, with no real integral.
The following section constructs the additive character sum on an odd finite
field and its elimination identity. The section after that identifies the
Euler product of every nondegenerate integral form with an
#{L}-function of its discriminant character. The last section sums the
Epstein zetas of the classes of a negative fundamental discriminant.
The Weil index is outside that identity. This chapter does not identify
the character sum with the real normalizer.}

